                "Deterministic CLV models compute customer value using static historical averages across cohort populations. The classic infinite-horizon customer retention formulation, codified by Paul Farris and Robert Blattberg, calculates CLV using three primary financial parameters: the periodic contribution margin m (gross margin dollars generated per customer per period, subtracting direct cost of goods sold and variable servicing costs), the customer retention rate r (the statistical probability that an active customer remains active into the subsequent period), and the enterprise discount rate d (the firm's Weighted Average Cost of Capital, or WACC, typically ranging between 8% and 14% annually). Under the assumption of constant margins and constant retention probabilities over an infinite time horizon, the expected present value converges through a geometric series to the elegant closed-form equation: CLV equals m multiplied by r, divided by the quantity one plus d minus r. This mathematical relationship reveals that customer value is profoundly non-linear: as the retention rate r approaches unity, the denominator contracts rapidly, causing customer lifetime value to surge exponentially.",
