                "Price Elasticity of Demand (PED, or epsilon) measures the responsiveness of customer purchase quantity to changes in unit price. Formulated by Alfred Marshall, point price elasticity is mathematically defined as the percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in price: epsilon = (dQ / dP) * (P / Q). Because demand curves almost universally slope downward (with rare exceptions such as Veblen luxury status goods and Giffen staples), elasticity is negative, though economists customarily discuss its absolute magnitude. When absolute elasticity is greater than 1.0, demand is Elastic: a 10% price increase triggers a volume contraction exceeding 10%, causing total gross revenue to decline. When absolute elasticity is less than 1.0, demand is Inelastic: a 10% price increase triggers a volume contraction of less than 10%, causing total gross revenue to expand. When absolute elasticity equals 1.0, demand is Unit Elastic, where total revenue reaches its mathematical inflection peak.",
                "In enterprise boardrooms, the ultimate measure of pricing power is the Lerner Index of Monopoly Power (L), formulated by Abba Lerner in 1934. The Lerner Index quantifies a firm's ability to price above marginal cost without suffering fatal volume attrition: L = (P - MC) / P, where P is the profit-maximizing selling price and MC is the marginal cost of production. Microeconomic optimization proves that for a profit-maximizing enterprise, the Lerner Index is inversely proportional to the absolute price elasticity of demand: L = 1 / |epsilon|. Rearranging this fundamental identity yields the classic Optimal Markup Rule: Profit-Maximizing Price P* = MC * [ |epsilon| / (|epsilon| - 1) ]. This equation demonstrates that as a brand establishes profound differentiation, brand loyalty, and high switching costs, customer price elasticity contracts toward zero (|epsilon| decreases), empowering the firm to expand gross profit margins indefinitely without fear of competitive substitution.",
